  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "An Individual Retirement Account (IRA) is a tax-advantaged personal savings account allowing individuals to invest for retirement, independent of employer-sponsored plans. They offer tax-deferred (Traditional) or tax-free (Roth) growth. Key requirements include having earned income, with 2026 contribution limits set at $7,500 ($8,600 for age 50+). How an IRA Works Traditional IRA: Contributions may be tax-deductible, reducing your current taxable income. Earnings grow tax-deferred, and withdrawals in retirement are taxed as ordinary income. Roth IRA: Contributions are made with after-tax dollars (no immediate deduction). However, earnings grow tax-free, and qualified withdrawals in retirement are entirely tax-free. Account Setup: IRAs are opened through financial institutions, such as banks, mutual fund companies, or stockbrokers. Eligibility and Contributions Eligibility: You must have earned income (wages, salaries, self-employment income) to contribute. Contribution Limits (2026): The total limit for 2026 is $7,500 per year, or $8,600 if you are age 50 or older. Income Limits: While anyone can open a traditional IRA, eligibility to contribute to a Roth IRA or deduct traditional IRA contributions depends on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI). How to Open an IRA Choose a Provider: Select a brokerage, bank, or robo-advisor. Select IRA Type: Choose between Traditional or Roth based on your tax situation. Open the Account: Complete an application online or in person. Fund the Account: Transfer money from a bank account or roll over funds from a 401(k). Invest the Funds: Select stocks, bonds, mutual funds, or ETFs within the account. Fees and Disadvantages Fees: Potential fees include annual maintenance fees, trading commissions, or expense ratios for mutual funds/ETFs. Disadvantages: Withdrawal Penalties: Withdrawing earnings or contributions (for traditional) before age 59½ often triggers a 10% penalty plus taxes. Contribution Limits: Lower annual limits compared to 401(k) plans. No Employer Match: Unlike 401(k)s, you do not get matching contributions from an employer. Other Types of IRAs SEP IRA: For self-employed individuals or small business owners, allowing higher contribution limits. SIMPLE IRA: A retirement plan for small businesses that allows both employer and employee contributions.",
